<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Claus Sluter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–1405/06</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Claus Sluter was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sculptor from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a> from 1385 for Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-bold">Philip the Bold</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. His figures for the portal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charterhouse-of-champmol">Charterhouse of Champmol</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well-of-moses">Well of Moses</a>, with their weight, movement and realistic faces, broke with the elegant International Gothic and shaped northern European sculpture. He also began the duke's tomb, completed after his death in 1405 or 1406.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claus-sluter.jpg" alt="Claus Sluter" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dijon_-_Puits_de_Mo%C3%AFse_-_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
